# The := slice (the author's correction of 2026-11-07; the book, assign-known): a method's name as the left side -- `one := two`
# in a method -- is refused at translation, located: the name resolves to the unit's callable field, which a method cannot
# write today (the existing limit "writing a hidden input that has no activation value is not supported yet"); nothing is
# called.  Inside a method the head `one` is a call head (l2_call_head_method), and only the `:=` branch of
# lm_compiler_is_asgn reads the statement as the write it is, so that the check says this refusal instead of binding a call
# of `one` with the actual `two` (mutant m2 of the slice survived the fixtures without this row).
fn: one () int
    return: 1
end: one
fn: two () int
    return: 2
end: two
fn: f () int
    one := two
    return: 1
end: f
int: code 7
if: f() != 1
    code: 81
---
send: exit(exit_code: code; stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
